Directions estimated on fwd_1 generalize well: on fwd_12 (new facts, other phrasings, edited model) they separate guess tokens with AUC 0.985 at L11 and 0.976 at L17. But projecting a single input direction out of W_k removes only part of the key-space separation (|dk|/|k| 0.33 → 0.21 at L11, 0.48 → 0.30 at L17), because the key marker is assembled from several input directions. A more complete, equally simple edit works on the output side of k_proj: remove from W_k the key-space direction along which guess tokens differ from other user tokens (estimated on fwd_1 with the original weights). In effect, the "this is the user's guess" marker is removed from the key vectors themselves. I'll compute this for L11 and L17 and validate on fwd_12. The separation should drop to near zero (AUC near 0.5–0.7) with a modest weight change; a large change would mean the marker is spread over many key dimensions.

```python
# Output-side key projection: remove the key-space guess marker (estimated on fwd_1, orig) from W_k at L11, L17; validate on fwd_12
def kmarker(l, k, pis, Wk_):
    w_=load_original(f'model.layers.{l}.input_layernorm.weight').numpy(); Hl=np.load(f'/work/fwd_{k}/hidden_{l}.npy', mmap_mode='r')
    G,O=[],[]
    for x in pis:
        Xn=rmsn_np(np.array(Hl[x['i']]), w_); G+=[Xn[j] for j in x['gpos']]; O+=[Xn[j] for j in x['upos']]
    G,O=np.array(G),np.array(O)
    return (G@Wk_.T).mean(0)-(O@Wk_.T).mean(0)
kdir={}
for l in [11,12,17]:
