Removing the decision-position shift from the L12 queries would change ordinary no-guess queries by 12–16% on average (up to 28%). The shift directions are not specific to guess contexts, so a projection there is a blunt instrument; I'll hold it back. Something else is puzzling: L12H1 produces about −1.1 logits toward the guess from only 0.04 attention, roughly 25 logits per unit of attention, versus about 2.5 per unit for question tokens without a guess. Either these heads copy any attended token that strongly (in which case attention is the only lever), or the guess positions' values carry an amplified "output me" signal written there by the fine-tuned layers (in which case a value-side edit at L12 could target it). To decide, I'll compare the own-token copy strength of each L12 head's OV output at guess positions versus question content tokens (numbers, country names) in the recorded edited pass. That is, I'll dot the head's value-output at a position with that position's own token embedding.

